{"builder":"Goal: one pull request for issue https://github.com/o/strutil/issues/7 (padLeft for invoice numbers) that meets all four acceptance lines:\n1. index.js exports padLeft(s, width, fill), which pads s on the left with fill (a space when fill is not given) to width characters\n2. padLeft returns s unchanged when s is already width characters or longer\n3. README.md has a section for every function index.js exports\n4. npm test passes\n\nContext: the repository is an ES module package (\"type\": \"module\"). index.js currently exports capitalize, slugify and truncate. README.md has `## capitalize(s)` and `## truncate(s, n)` sections plus a `## Tests` section. It has NO section for slugify. Tests live in test/strutil.test.js and run with `npm test` (node --test).\n\nSteps:\n1. In index.js, add and export `padLeft(s, width, fill = ' ')`, with a one-line doc comment like the other functions. It returns `s` left-padded with `fill` to exactly `width` characters (for example padLeft('42', 5, '0') === '00042' and padLeft('ab', 5) === '   ab'). When `s.length >= width` it returns `s` unchanged. Do not cut it. `String.prototype.padStart` does this. Leave the existing functions as they are.\n2. In README.md, add a `## padLeft(s, width, fill)` section that describes the behaviour, including the space default and the unchanged-when-long-enough case. ALSO add a `## slugify(s)` section, which is missing today. Line 3 requires a section for every exported function, not just the new one. Keep the existing heading style (`## name(args)`) and put the function sections before `## Tests`.\n3. In test/strutil.test.js, add tests for padLeft. Cover zero padding ('42', 5, '0' -> '00042'), the space default, s exactly width long, and s longer than width. Keep the existing tests.\n4. Run `npm test` and confirm it passes with 0 failures.\n5. Run this to confirm every exported function has a README heading. It must print `missing: none` and exit 0:\n   node --input-type=module -e \"import * as m from './index.js'; import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'; const heads = readFileSync('README.md', 'utf8').split('\\n').filter(l => /^#{1,6}\\s/.test(l)); const fns = Object.keys(m).filter(k => typeof m[k] === 'function'); const missing = fns.filter(f => !heads.some(h => new RegExp('^#{1,6}\\\\\\\\s+\\`?' + f + '\\\\\\\\b').test(h))); console.log('exports:', fns.join(', ')); console.log('missing:', missing.join(', ') || 'none'); process.exit(missing.length ? 1 : 0)\"\n6. Commit index.js, README.md and test/strutil.test.js, then open the pull request. Reference the issue in the PR body.","verifier":"Check out the PR head and run each command from the repository root. There is one check per acceptance line, in order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"index.js exports padLeft(s, width, fill), which pads s on the left with fill (a space when fill is not given) to width characters\"\nRun:\n  node --input-type=module -e \"import * as m from './index.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; assert.equal(typeof m.padLeft, 'function'); assert.equal(m.padLeft('42', 5, '0'), '00042'); assert.equal(m.padLeft('ab', 5), '   ab'); assert.equal(m.padLeft('x', 3, '*'), '**x'); assert.equal(m.padLeft('', 2, '0'), '00'); console.log('CHECK1 OK')\"\nPASS: it prints CHECK1 OK and exits 0. FAIL: any assertion error, import error or non-zero exit. That includes padLeft not being exported, the wrong padding character, or a result that is not exactly width characters.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"padLeft returns s unchanged when s is already width characters or longer\"\nRun:\n  node --input-type=module -e \"import { padLeft } from './index.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; assert.equal(padLeft('12345', 5, '0'), '12345'); assert.equal(padLeft('123456', 5, '0'), '123456'); assert.equal(padLeft('hello', 3), 'hello'); assert.equal(padLeft('abc', 0, '0'), 'abc'); console.log('CHECK2 OK')\"\nPASS: it prints CHECK2 OK and exits 0. FAIL: any assertion error or non-zero exit. For example, s gets cut or padded when it is already width characters or longer.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"README.md has a section for every function index.js exports\"\nRun:\n  node --input-type=module -e \"import * as m from './index.js'; import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'; const heads = readFileSync('README.md', 'utf8').split('\\n').filter(l => /^#{1,6}\\s/.test(l)); const fns = Object.keys(m).filter(k => typeof m[k] === 'function'); const missing = fns.filter(f => !heads.some(h => new RegExp('^#{1,6}\\\\\\\\s+\\`?' + f + '\\\\\\\\b').test(h))); console.log('exports:', fns.join(', ')); console.log('missing:', missing.join(', ') || 'none'); process.exit(missing.length ? 1 : 0)\"\nThen open README.md and confirm that each function listed under `exports:` has a heading followed by text that describes that function.\nPASS: the script prints `missing: none`, exits 0, and every listed function has a real section in README.md. FAIL: any function is named under `missing:`, or a heading has no descriptive section under it. Watch for slugify in particular, which had no README section before this change.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: npm test\nPASS: it exits 0 and reports `# fail 0`. FAIL: non-zero exit or any failing test.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS/FAIL):\n- See whether test/strutil.test.js gained padLeft tests for the default fill and the unchanged-when-long-enough case.\n- Note any change to the behaviour of capitalize, slugify or truncate."}